@@ -61,6 +61,52 @@ where a domain is in use, the A-label form can be presented when it
 is not a valid IDN.
 
 
+### ASCII labels and case
+
+That rule governs labels this library has to convert. A label that is
+already all-ASCII needs no conversion, so `encode()` checks it and
+returns it as it stands, with its case intact - it neither rejects the
+capitals nor folds them:
+
+```pycon
+>>> import idna
+>>> idna.encode('EXAMPLE.COM')
+b'EXAMPLE.COM'
+>>> idna.encode('EXAMPLE.COM', uts46=True)
+b'example.com'
+```
+
+Folding case is UTS #46's job, so pass `uts46=True` where two spellings
+of one name should encode alike. `idna.check_label()`, by contrast,
+applies IDNA 2008 to the label as given and does reject uppercase, so it
+and `encode()` differ on an all-ASCII label.
+
+
+### Codec
+
+The conversions are also available through the standard codec
+machinery under the name `idna2008`. Importing `idna.codec` is what
+registers it, so that import is required even where the name is the
+only thing used afterwards:
+
+```pycon
+>>> import codecs
+>>> import idna.codec
+>>> codecs.encode('ドメイン.テスト', 'idna2008')
+b'xn--eckwd4c7c.xn--zckzah'
+>>> codecs.decode(b'xn--eckwd4c7c.xn--zckzah', 'idna2008')
+'ドメイン.テスト'
+>>> 'ドメイン.テスト'.encode('idna2008')
+b'xn--eckwd4c7c.xn--zckzah'
+```
+
+The codec also provides the incremental and stream forms of the
+protocol, so a domain arriving in pieces is converted one whole label
+at a time. Only the `strict` error handler is supported; any other
+handler raises `idna.IDNAError` rather than silently dropping or
+replacing characters.
+
+
 ## Exceptions
 
 All errors raised during conversion derive from the `idna.IDNAError`
